---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="NH Small Claims — case study — JPX Web Development Co."
  description="Self-help website for New Hampshire small claims court, built for a Dartmouth College student volunteer project."
>
  <div class="max-w-[760px] mx-auto px-8 pt-16 pb-24">
    <a href="/#work" class="link-ghost text-[14px] mb-8">
      <span class="arr" style="transform: rotate(180deg);">→</span> Back to work
    </a>

    <span class="tag">published</span>
    <h1 class="text-[36px] md:text-[46px] mt-5 mb-3">NH Small Claims</h1>
    <p class="text-[18px] max-w-[46ch] mb-8" style="color: var(--ink-soft);">
      Free, plain-language self-help guide for New Hampshire small claims court — built for a Dartmouth College
      student volunteer project.
    </p>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-10">
      <a href="https://nhsmallclaims.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="tag" style="text-decoration: none;">
        nhsmallclaims.org ↗
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="project-card glass mb-10">
      <div class="browser-chrome">
        <div class="browser-chrome__bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>nhsmallclaims.org</span></div>
        <div class="card-shot">
          <img
            src="https://api.microlink.io/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnhsmallclaims.org&screenshot=true&meta=false&embed=screenshot.url"
            alt="NH Small Claims homepage"
            loading="lazy"
            style="object-fit: cover;"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-case">
      <h2>The org</h2>
      <p>
        NH Small Claims is a volunteer project run by Dartmouth College students, aimed at making New Hampshire's
        small claims process easier to navigate for people representing themselves — no lawyer, no legal advice,
        just clear, plain-language information kept up to date in one place.
      </p>

      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Small claims court is designed to be usable without a lawyer, but "designed to be" and "actually is" are
        different things — the process, forms, and terminology are scattered across sources that assume you
        already know how courts work. The site's job is to close that gap for whoever's filing a claim or just got
        served with one.
      </p>

      <h2>What I built</h2>
      <p>
        A resource site structured around the actual questions people show up with: an FAQ covering filing,
        responding, hearings, and appeals; a court lookup to find the right District Division court for your town;
        a resources section for mediation services and free or low-cost legal aid; and an About page explaining who
        runs the site and why. The homepage leads with exactly what it helps with — who can file, what happens
        after you're served, how judgments get collected — so people can tell in ten seconds whether they're in the
        right place.
      </p>

      <h2>Impact</h2>
      <p>
        A student volunteer team now has a live, working legal self-help resource for an entire state's small
        claims process — not a mockup or a class project sitting unused, but a real site real people can use to
        figure out what to do next.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 flex items-center gap-5">
      <a href="/contact" class="btn-neu inline-block">Book a 20-min call</a>
      <a class="link-ghost" href="/#work">More work <span class="arr">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
